From: Dallas Delaney <dadelan@microsoft.com>
Subject: [PATCH] erofs: bind dm-verity enforcement to the selected applier

Single commit on the ACL containerd branch:

  erofs: bind dm-verity enforcement to the selected applier


  Discovery of dm-verity referrers was derived from any *loaded* diff plugin
  advertising CapabilityDmverityReferrers, but layers are applied by the differ
  that is actually *selected*. The transfer service takes its differ from
  unpack_config, while the CRI local pull path takes it from the diff service,
  whose default order is walking-only. When CRI falls back to local pull the two
  disagree: artifacts are fetched and discarded, layers are applied with no
  dm-verity device, and configuration still reads as though verification is on.
  The downgrade is reported only as a warning.

  The fallback is not hypothetical. CheckLocalImagePullConfigs flips CRI to local
  pull for several ordinary settings -- disable_snapshot_annotations, registry
  mirrors and configs, max_concurrent_downloads -- so an unrelated node
  configuration change can silently disable verification.

  Report the selected applier chain from the diff service and fail plugin
  initialisation when discovery is enabled but nothing in that chain can consume
  the artifacts, rather than degrading silently. Warn when a capable differ is
  present but not first, since an earlier differ may still apply the layer.

  Also:

    - IsSupported() only read /proc/modules, so a CONFIG_DM_VERITY=y kernel
      reported unsupported and disabled the feature entirely. Check
      /sys/module/dm_verity as well, which is present for built-in targets.
      Reword the corresponding mount error, which named a missing module as the
      only possible cause.

    - The shared layer SELinux label was hardcoded to a distro-specific type
      while its own comment cited a different type as precedent. Make it
      configurable via shared_layer_context, keep the validated value as the
      default, and correct the comment to say which type is used and why.


diff --git a/internal/dmverity/dmverity_linux.go b/internal/dmverity/dmverity_linux.go
index a3be00c4b..abc16f6ad 100644
--- a/internal/dmverity/dmverity_linux.go
+++ b/internal/dmverity/dmverity_linux.go
@@ -31,16 +31,37 @@ import (
 	"github.com/google/uuid"
 )
 
+// IsSupported reports whether the kernel can back a dm-verity device.
+//
+// A false return with a nil error means the answer is determinate: dm-verity
+// is genuinely unavailable, and callers may skip cleanly. A non-nil error
+// means the answer is indeterminate — the check itself could not be
+// completed — and callers should treat that as a hard failure rather than
+// assume absence.
 func IsSupported() (bool, error) {
-	moduleData, err := os.ReadFile("/proc/modules")
-	if err != nil {
+	// dm-verity may be a loadable module or built into the kernel. Checking
+	// only /proc/modules reports unsupported on a CONFIG_DM_VERITY=y kernel and
+	// silently disables verification, so consult the built-in target list too.
+	if moduleData, err := os.ReadFile("/proc/modules"); err == nil {
+		if bytes.Contains(moduleData, []byte("dm_verity")) {
+			return true, nil
+		}
+	} else if !os.IsNotExist(err) {
 		return false, fmt.Errorf("failed to read /proc/modules: %w", err)
 	}
-	if !bytes.Contains(moduleData, []byte("dm_verity")) {
-		return false, fmt.Errorf("dm_verity module not loaded")
+
+	// /sys/module/dm_verity is present for a built-in target as well as a
+	// loaded module, so it covers CONFIG_DM_VERITY=y kernels that never
+	// appear in /proc/modules.
+	if _, err := os.Stat("/sys/module/dm_verity"); err == nil {
+		return true, nil
+	} else if !os.IsNotExist(err) {
+		return false, fmt.Errorf("failed to stat /sys/module/dm_verity: %w", err)
 	}
 
-	return true, nil
+	// Both probes succeeded and neither found dm-verity, so this is a
+	// determinate "not available" rather than a failed check.
+	return false, nil
 }
 
 func convertToVerityParams(opts *DmverityOptions) (verity.Params, error) {
